\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Capabilities of motion generators across skeletons, as described in the papers and released code. \emph  {Any topology}: one model serves skeletons of different joint counts and trees. \emph  {Text}: language-conditioned. \emph  {Rotations}: joint rotations are predicted. \emph  {IK-free}: the exported animation needs no inverse-kinematics fit. \emph  {Root}: a world-space root trajectory is generated. \emph  {Unseen rig}: a rig absent from training is served, and how. \emph  {Baselines}: how the paper compares to other methods. n.r.\ = not reported. SAMoR is its cross-topology variant; its HumanML3D variant has a root and rotation branch. G-MDM omits root translation in its current implementation.}}{16}{table.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:capability}{{6}{16}{Capabilities of motion generators across skeletons, as described in the papers and released code. \emph {Any topology}: one model serves skeletons of different joint counts and trees. \emph {Text}: language-conditioned. \emph {Rotations}: joint rotations are predicted. \emph {IK-free}: the exported animation needs no inverse-kinematics fit. \emph {Root}: a world-space root trajectory is generated. \emph {Unseen rig}: a rig absent from training is served, and how. \emph {Baselines}: how the paper compares to other methods. n.r.\ = not reported. SAMoR is its cross-topology variant; its HumanML3D variant has a root and rotation branch. G-MDM omits root translation in its current implementation}{table.6}{}}
